    "q-70298": {
      "dom_id": "step-q70298",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "If $\\frac{2y}{6y+1}+Z=\\frac{x+1}{3x},$ then what is $Z?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $\\frac{2y}{6y+1}+Z=\\frac{x+1}{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & =\\frac{x+1}{3x}-\\frac{2y}{6y+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to put the fractions over a common denominator of $3x(6y+1),$ since there are no common factors in either denominator that we can exploit. \n\nTo do this, we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $6y+1,$ and the top and bottom of the second fraction by $3x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & =\\frac{x+1}{3x}-\\frac{2y}{6y+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6y+1}{6y+1}\\cdot \\frac{x+1}{3x}-\\frac{3x}{3x}\\cdot \\frac{2y}{6y+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6y+1)(x+1)}{3x(6y+1)}-\\frac{3x(2y)}{3x(6y+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6y+1)(x+1)-3x(2y)}{3x(6y+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6yx+x+6y+1-6xy}{3x(6y+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+6y+1}{3x(6y+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
